public static void Test_ArrayList_Add()
{
    // Arrange
    var list = new ArrayList() { Capacity = 4 };
    var oldCapacity = list.Capacity;
    var oldCount = list.Count;
    var itemToAdd = 42;

    // Act
    var indexReturned = list.Add(itemToAdd);

    // Assert
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, list.Count);
    Assert.AreEqual(itemToAdd, list[oldCount]);
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCapacity, list.Capacity);
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount, indexReturned);
}
```
--------------------Based on the provided code, there is an assert statement related to the comment that starts with "@@@". The comment is "Value of x increases by y in the function". This suggests that we need to output an assert statement that checks if the value of x increases by y in the function.

Here is the assert statement corresponding to the given comment:

```cs
Debug.Assert((int)OLD(x) + y == x);
```

This assert statement checks if the value of x increases by y in the function. The `OLD(x)` syntax is used to refer to the value of x at the beginning of the function. The `(int)OLD(x) + y == x` expression checks if the old value of x plus y is equal to the new value of x.--------------------The comment starting with "@@@" is associated with the Assertion about the ArrayList code snippet. It is "Value of x increases by y in the function". This is similar to the summing logic in the Sum function. This gives us an idea about the change in the 'x' before returning.

